1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Bc4 d5 4. exd5 Bg4 5. d3 Nbd7 6. h3 Bh5 7. g4 Bg6 8. Qe2 Bd6 9. Nc3 O-O 10. Ne4 Nxe4 11. dxe4 a6 12. Bd3 Nc5 13. h4 f6 14. h5 Nxd3+ 15. Qxd3 Be8 16. Nh4 c6 17. Nf5 c5 18. Be3 h6 19. Qd2 Rf7 {Up to the position upon the diagram the Oxonian had the best of the encounter, and should have won the game.} 20. Bxh6 gxh6 21. Nxh6+ Kh7 22. Nxf7 Bxf7 {... etc, 0-1, 52 moves. "Of course, he might have dispensed with the sacrifice; but it is very tempting and might be condoned. After all, he got three pawns and a rook for two bishops, and should not have lost the game. But he mismanaged it afterwards, and resigned on his 52nd move." Source: The Field, 1 April 1893, p495} 0-1

