1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6 4. d3 Bc5 5. Be3 Bxe3 6. fxe3 d5 7. exd5 Nxd5 8. Qd2 O-O 9. Nc3 Nf6 10. e4 Nd4 {"Probably an oversight." (Hoffer, The Field)} 11. Nxe5 Qd6 12. Nf3 Bg4 13. Nxd4 Qxd4 14. h3 Be6 15. Bxe6 fxe6 16. O-O-O Qb4 17. Qg5 Kh8 18. Ne2 Qa4 19. Kb1 Qe8 20. Rhf1 e5 21. Ng3 h6 22. Qe3 Qg6 23. Nf5 Rf7 24. g4 Nd7 25. Qg3 Raf8 26. h4 b6 27. g5 c6 28. Ne3 Rf4 29. Nf5 Rxf1 30. Rxf1 Kh7 31. Qh3 Qf7 32. Rg1 Kh8 {"The Oxonian took a considerable time to make this move, which is, however, the best. Time being called, the game was adjudicated by Mr Hoffer in favour of Cambridge. Black is a pawn behind, with an isolated king’s pawn, and White has only to move his QR or QKtP so as to have an outlet for the king, and he should finally win." (Hoffer, The Field) (Sources: The Field, 1 April 1893, p496; Chess Monthly, Vol. 14, p241)} 1-0

